error[SE0205]: `double` takes one unit argument, found 0
  --> tests/err/units.se:10:12
   |
10 |         a: double;               // a scalar takes exactly one unit argument
   |            ^^^^^^ expected `double(unit)`
   = note: the unit is not optional; `(-)` spells dimensionless (§5.1)
   = help: write `setting: double(-)` if it really is dimensionless

error[SE0206]: malformed unit expression: expected a unit symbol, found `)`
  --> tests/err/units.se:11:21
   |
11 |         b: double(m/)    = 1.0;  // dangling operator
   |                     ^ expected a unit symbol
   = note: a unit is `1`, a symbol, or a parenthesised unit expression, combined with `*`, `/` and `^` (§4.1)

error[SE0206]: unit exponent must be an integer
  --> tests/err/units.se:12:21
   |
12 |         c: double(m^1.5) = 1.0;  // non-integer exponent
   |                     ^^^
   = note: rational exponents are written `m^(1/2)` (§4.1)

error[SE0207]: unknown scalar type `dobule`
  --> tests/err/units.se:13:12
   |
13 |         d: dobule(m)     = 1.0;  // typo in the scalar type
   |            ^^^^^^
   = help: did you mean `double`?
   = note: the scalar type set is closed: `double`, `float`, `int`, `bool` (§5.1)

error[SE0206]: only the literal `1` may appear in a unit expression
  --> tests/err/units.se:14:19
   |
14 |         e: double(2*m)   = 1.0;  // only `1` may appear as a literal
   |                   ^ found `2`
   = note: `1` is the identity unit, as in `(1/(kg*m^2))` (§4.1)

error[SE0205]: `bool` takes no unit argument, found 1
  --> tests/err/units.se:15:16
   |
15 |         g: bool(-)       = 1;    // `bool` takes no unit argument
   |                ^ expected `bool`
   = note: `bool` takes no unit: a scaled or dimensioned truth value is meaningless (§5.1)

